{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of volatility: The model consistently predicts a smooth, monotonic trend (either flat or slightly upward) while true outcomes exhibit high-amplitude spikes (e.g., rising to 76+) followed by sharp declines. Apply a volatility multiplier to forecast ranges rather than point estimates.",
    "Implement aggressive mean reversion after sentiment spikes: When sentiment history shows a value of 1.00 (positive) or -1.00 (negative), the model fails to anticipate the subsequent price reversal. If sentiment is positive, immediately reduce the forecast slope or apply a downward correction in the following 5-10 days to account for the inevitable pullback.",
    "Eliminate forecast lag on trend reversals: The model reacts too slowly to turning points, often continuing a previous trend direction for several days after the true price has already reversed. Shift the forecast horizon to react to the most recent 3-day price momentum rather than the full 18-day history.",
    "Adjust for 'overshoot' bias: The model systematically underestimates the peak magnitude of rallies (e.g., predicting ~72 when the true peak is ~76) and overestimates the floor of corrections. Widen the forecast confidence interval to capture these extreme deviations.",
    "Decouple long-term forecasts from short-term sentiment noise: The model's 30-day projection is overly sensitive to the immediate past price level, failing to account for structural shifts. Reduce the weight of the most recent 5 days of history when the sentiment history contains conflicting signals (e.g., alternating 1.00 and -1.00)."
  ]
}
